nd zero otherwise. • εi,h: idiosyncratic cost shock to product i, mean zero conditional on (H(i), o(i))."
R2-102,R2,Bank of Canada (2026). The price impact of Canadian retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-22),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/swp2026-22.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"te for products with o(i) = US and H(i) ∈T , and zero otherwise. • εi,h: idiosyncratic cost shock to product i, mean zero conditional on (H(i), o(i)). Sets and indicators: • T : set of HS8 codes targeted by the March 4, 2025 Canadian retaliatory tariffs. • Ti ≡1{H(i) ∈T }: indicator that product i’s HS code is on the tariff list. Varies at the HS8 level. Structural parameters: • ρ ∈(0, 1]: pass-through of own marginal cost to price (cost-side elasticity). • Ω∈[0, 1): strategic complementarity in price-setting — the elasticity of firm i’s price to the average price in its HS code, holding own cost fixed. Cell averages: • ∆pH,h ≡E[∆pj,h | H(j) = H]: equilibrium average log price change in HS code H at horizon h, taken across all countries of origin in the cell. 19 Fixed effects: • γc,h: COICOP × horizon (or COICOP × day) fixed effect. • ζr,h: retailer × horizon fixed effect. Pricing equation. The marginal cost change for product i decomposes as ∆mci,h = δH(i),h + αo(i),h + τi,h + εi,h, (F.1) and prices reflect own marginal cost and the equilibrium price in the firm’s competitive set, which we take to be its HS code: ∆pi,h = ρ · ∆mci,h + Ω· ∆pH(i),h. (F.2) Substituting (F.1) into (F.2) gives ∆pi,h = ρ δH(i),h + ρ αo(i),h + ρ τi,h + Ω∆pH(i),h + ρ εi,h. (F.3) Identification strategy. We now show that ρ is identified by a double-difference-in- differences using all four cells of the product taxonomy."
