R2-104,R2,Bank of Canada (2026). The price impact of Canadian retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-22),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/swp2026-22.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,". (F.3) Identification strategy. We now show that ρ is identified by a double-difference-in- differences using all four cells of the product taxonomy. Taking expectations within each cell at horizon h, (F.3) gives the structural equations for the four cells: E[∆pi,h | o(i) = US, H(i) ∈T ] = ρ ( ¯δT ,h + αUS,h + τ) + Ω∆pT ,h, (F.4) E[∆pi,h | o(i) = US, H(i) /∈T ] = ρ ( ¯δ¬T ,h + αUS,h) + Ω∆p¬T ,h, (F.5) E[∆pi,h | o(i) = CAN, H(i) ∈T ] = ρ ( ¯δT ,h + αCAN,h) + Ω∆pT ,h, (F.6) E[∆pi,h | o(i) = CAN, H(i) /∈T ] = ρ ( ¯δ¬T ,h + αCAN,h) + Ω∆p¬T ,h, (F.7) where ¯δT ,h ≡E[δH,h | H ∈T ] and ¯δ¬T ,h defined analogously. We refer to the four cells, in order, as Tariffed (T), US NonSubstitutes (USNS), Domestic Substitutes (DS), and Domestic NonSubstitutes (DNS). The baseline LP-DiD comparison in our main specification uses Tariffed as treatment and Domestic NonSubstitutes as control. The cell-mean difference is E[∆pi,h | T] −E[∆pi,h | DNS] = ρτ + ρ (αUS,h −αCAN,h) | {z } country-of-origin gap + ρ ( ¯δT ,h −¯δ¬T ,h) | {z } HS-selection gap + Ω(∆pT ,h −∆p¬T ,h) | {z } spillover . (F.8) 20 The single-DiD coefficient ˆβTariffed h recovers a sum of ρτ and three additional terms, due to non-random assignment of country of origin, non-random assignment of HS category, and strategic pricing complementarities. The four-cell structure allows construction of a difference-in-differences that nets out both nuisance terms simultaneously. Define ∆T ,h ≡E[∆p | T] −E[∆p | DS], ∆¬T ,h ≡E[∆p | USNS] −E[∆p | DNS]. From (F.4)–(F.7): ∆T ,h = ρτ + ρ (αUS,h −αCAN,h), (F.9) ∆¬T ,h = ρ (αUS,h −αCAN,h). (F.10) The within-HS differences ¯δT ,h and ¯δ¬T ,h cancel within each bracket because both terms share the same HS code. The spillover terms Ω∆pT ,h and Ω∆p¬T ,h also cancel within each bracket, because the cell average depends only on H(i) ∈T , not on o(i). The double- difference is then ∆T ,h −∆¬T ,h = ρτ. (F.11) ρ is identified as ˆρh = (∆T ,h −∆¬T ,h)/τ, with τ taken as the known statutory tariff rate. The identifying assumption."
