R2-118,R2,Bank of Canada (2026). The price impact of Canadian retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-22),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/swp2026-22.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"that is the case, the tariff is expected to push retail prices up by more. Conversely, products with a low U.S. share of imports already have non-U.S. alternatives that can absorb the shock, so the tariff should translate into a smaller retail price response. In this Section, we analyze this channel by comparing the effects of the retaliatory tariffs on retail prices of products with high vs. low pre-tariff exposure to U.S. imports. For each HS8 product code, we measure the U.S. share of total Canadian imports pre- tariff (variable share value hs8 in the panel) based on Canadian International Merchan- dise Trade data.11 We define U.S.-import exposure dummy as dUS i = 1{share value hs8i ≥median(share value hs8)} , with the median taken across the set of unique HS8 codes that appear in the panel (so each HS8 enters the cutoff once, regardless of how many products map to it). Products with dUS i = 1 are the “high U.S. share” subgroup—those that, pre-tariff, sourced an above- median share of Canadian imports from the United States. The remaining treated prod- ucts form the “low U.S. share” subgroup with dUS i = 0."
