R2-127,R2,Bank of Canada (2026). The price impact of Canadian retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-22),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/swp2026-22.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"f inventory across the assigned NAICS retailers in the third column. Figure K1. Time-to-peak pass-through versus days of inventory: COICOP categories. Note: Horizontal axis: mean days of inventory across the NAICS retailers assigned to each COICOP (Ta- ble K2). Vertical axis: hpeak (horizon at which the LP-DiD coefficient reaches its maximum in Figure 5). Sample excludes COICOP 600 (Health) and 800 (Communication). Pearson correlation ρ = +0.354 (n = 6). 34 K.3 Within-Food evidence: product-level shelf life from USDA Food- Keeper So far, we used variation in average inventory stocks for retailers selling goods in dif- ferent 1-digit COICOP categories. In this Section, we exploit variation of perishability of food products, i.e., we use variation across products within a 1-digit COICOP category for Food. We use a product-level proxy of perishability from the USDA FoodKeeper dataset (https://www.foodsafety.gov/keep-food-safe/foodkeeper-app), which reports recom- mended shelf life for 661 individual food products in three storage modes (pantry, re- frigerate, freeze). We classified each product to one of the eleven food 3-digit COICOP categories using its name, sub-category, and keywords. For each 3-digit food COICOP we compute the mean refrigerate shelf life across its products."
