R4-000,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"Staff working paper / Document de travail du personnel—2026-35 Last updated: October 2, 2026 The Production Network Amplification of Retaliatory Tariffs Christian Bustamante Financial Stability Department Bank of Canada CBustamante@bank-banque-canada.ca Lu Han International Economic Analysis Department Bank of Canada LuHan@bank-banque-canada.ca Gaelan MacKenzie International Economic Analysis Department Bank of Canada GMacKenzie@bank-banque-canada.ca Xing Guo Canadian Economic Analysis Department Bank of Canada XingGuo@bank-banque-canada.ca Soyoung Lee Financial Stability Department Bank of Canada SoyoungLee@bank-banque-canada.ca Mariel Yacolca Maguiña marielyacolcam@gmail.com Jacob Short Financial Stability Department Bank of Canada JShort@bank-banque-canada.ca"
R4-001,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"anque-canada.ca Mariel Yacolca Maguiña marielyacolcam@gmail.com Jacob Short Financial Stability Department Bank of Canada JShort@bank-banque-canada.ca Bank of Canada staff research is produced independently from the Bank’s Governing Council and may support or challenge prevailing views. The views expressed in this paper are solely those of the authors and may differ from official Bank of Canada positions. No responsibility for them should be attributed to the Bank. DOI: https://doi.org/10.34989/swp-2026-35 | ISSN 1701-9397 © 2026 Bank of Canada The Production Network Amplification of Retaliatory Tariffs* Christian Bustamante, Xing Guo, Lu Han, Soyoung Lee, Gaelan MacKenzie, Mariel Yacolca Maguiña, and Jacob Short† Bank of Canada July 2026 Abstract We develop a framework to quantify how tariff changes on imported goods affect sectoral and aggregate consumption prices through direct and production-network channels. Using Canada’s input-output matrix and administrative firm-good import data for the universe of Canadian firms, we estimate price elasticities for tariffs on U.S.-imported goods. Tariffs on machinery and equipment and transport equipment generate the largest effects on final prices, reflecting their widespread use as intermediate inputs. On average, two-thirds of the total price response stems from higher intermediate input costs passing through domestic supply chains."
R4-002,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"rmediate inputs. On average, two-thirds of the total price response stems from higher intermediate input costs passing through domestic supply chains. Applying the framework to Canada’s 2025 counter-tariffs, we estimate consumption price increases of 1.56 and 0.72 percentage points for the pre- and post-September 2025 phases, respectively; alternative packages of equal import value could reduce aggregate price effects by three to seven times. A hypothetical CUSMA breakdown reverting to MFN tariffs would raise final-good prices by 0.64 percentage points. JEL: F13, F14, D57, E31 *The views expressed in this paper are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect the official views of the Bank of Canada or its Governing Council. †Corresponding author: Jacob Short, Bank of Canada, 234 Wellington St W, Ottawa, ON, Canada K1A 0G9, 343-574-1762, jshort@bank-banque-canada.ca 1 1 Introduction Recent trade tensions have spurred new analysis of the effect of increased tariffs on prices and economic activity. In March and April 2025, the U.S. announced increased tariffs for a significant fraction of its imports. Countries subject to these tariffs have responded with counter-tariffs on U.S.-imported goods. For example, Canada announced a series of retaliatory measures imposing a 25 percent tariff on a number of goods imported from the U.S. In September 2025, some of these tariffs were lifted, but an important fraction of tariffs on steel, aluminium, and motor vehicles remained. In parallel, the Canada-U.S.- Mexico trade agreement (CUSMA/USMCA) is due to be reviewed in 2026, in a context where tariffs could undergo further substantial changes. We propose a framework to quantify the impact of tariff changes on sectoral and aggregate final consumption prices. Tariffs are levied on goods, which can affect consumer prices through two channels."
R4-003,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"tariff changes on sectoral and aggregate final consumption prices. Tariffs are levied on goods, which can affect consumer prices through two channels. First, goods consumed directly by households experience immediate price changes following a tariff shock. Second, goods used as intermediate inputs in production transmit cost increases downstream, ultimately accumulating into the prices of final goods. We exploit the production network structure embedded in the economy’s input-output matrix to capture both the direct and indirect effects of tariffs on final consumption prices. To identify which goods are affected and measure these effects precisely, we use detailed administrative data at the firm-good level covering the universe of Canadian importers. We compute final price elasticities for tariffs at the level of U.S.-imported goods. To isolate the impact of tariff shocks, we assume that markups and non-intermediate input costs remain unaffected by tariffs."
R4-004,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"of U.S.-imported goods. To isolate the impact of tariff shocks, we assume that markups and non-intermediate input costs remain unaffected by tariffs. The linearity of the framework allows us to evaluate any trade scenario by aggregating good-level elasticities and to decompose total price effects into their direct and indirect components. These assumptions allow us to isolate the impact of tariff shocks, while recognizing that our measure provides an upper bound on these effects and that general equilibrium effects may mitigate the final impact. 2 Our findings show that tariffs on machinery and equipment and on transport equipment have the largest impact on final consumption prices. These are goods that have a large import share across different Canadian production sectors. Our results also show that, on average across different good categories, indirect effects account for about two- thirds of the total effect following tariff changes. The share explained by indirect effects depends on the centrality of sectors that import that particular good, as well as on how much of that sector’s production flows back into the economy’s supply chain rather than being used for final consumption. These results underscore the need to account for production network effects when quantifying the effects of tariff shocks. The pre- and post-September 2025 phases of Canadian counter-tariffs would have increased the price of final consumption by 1.56 and 0.72 percentage points, respectively."
R4-005,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"eptember 2025 phases of Canadian counter-tariffs would have increased the price of final consumption by 1.56 and 0.72 percentage points, respectively. This response reflects the direct-indirect composition of tariff elasticities: about two- thirds of the total effect is explained by production network effects. However, we also find that these tariff packages do not minimize the impact on aggregate prices. To show this, we compute the responses to alternative packages that levy tariffs affecting the same value of imports, but that would minimize the impact on aggregate prices. These alternative packages cut the aggregate impact by a margin between three and seven times. In the context of the ongoing CUSMA review process, we estimate the effects of a potential collapse of the agreement, under which Canadian tariffs on U.S. goods would revert to most-favored-nation rates. In this scenario, all U.S. imports would face tariffs, but for most goods, these rates would remain well below the 25 percent counter-tariffs announced in 2025. As a result, the total effect on final-good prices would be 0.64 percentage points."
R4-006,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"emain well below the 25 percent counter-tariffs announced in 2025. As a result, the total effect on final-good prices would be 0.64 percentage points. Finally, and in addition to our quantitative analysis, we construct import-level data that may be of independent interest. Given that our raw data contains good-level import data for the universe of Canadian firms, we compute the shares of U.S.-imported goods 3 used as intermediate inputs aggregated at the 2-digit NAICS level. This information could be exploited by researchers when conducting further analysis. Related Literature This paper speaks to three related strands of the literature. The first studies the incidence and welfare effects of recent tariff increases. Amiti, Redding and Weinstein (2019) and Fajgelbaum, Goldberg, Kennedy and Khandelwal (2020) show that the 2018–19 U.S. tariffs on Chinese goods were passed through almost one-for- one to import prices, implying that domestic consumers and firms bore most of the burden. Fajgelbaum, Goldberg, Kennedy, Khandelwal and Taglioni (2024) summarize this evidence and conclude that the trade war reduced real income in both the United States and China. More recently, focusing on Canada’s 2025 retaliatory tariffs on U.S."
R4-007,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"clude that the trade war reduced real income in both the United States and China. More recently, focusing on Canada’s 2025 retaliatory tariffs on U.S. goods, Cavallo, Kostyshyna, Kryvtsov and Vieyra (2026) document substantial retail- price pass-through for tariffed products, with limited spillovers to untariffed goods, providing new evidence on the transmission of retaliatory tariffs to consumer prices in a non-U.S. setting. The second literature emphasizes the role of supply chains and production networks in transmitting tariff shocks, and how that transmission goes beyond the direct effects on affected goods. Focusing again on the 2018–19 U.S.-China trade war, Cavallo, Gopinath, Neiman and Tang (2021) find that downstream retail-price adjustments were more muted than border-price adjustments, suggesting that margins and distribution sectors partly absorb tariff shocks. Handley, Monarch and Kamal (2024) show that tariffs disrupted buyer-supplier relationships and affected exports through imported-input linkages."
R4-008,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"ocks. Handley, Monarch and Kamal (2024) show that tariffs disrupted buyer-supplier relationships and affected exports through imported-input linkages. More broadly, Antràs and Chor (2022), Baqaee and Rubbo (2023), and Luo and Villar (2023) highlight that input-output linkages are central to the propagation of cost shocks across sectors and to their aggregate consequences. Our paper is closely related to this strand because it quantifies how tariff shocks propagate through Canada’s production network, rather than only through directly affected consumer goods. 4 The third, more recent literature quantifies the inflationary effects of tariffs using input-output methods. Barbiero and Stein (2025) show that tariff effects on prices depend not only on households’ direct purchases of imported goods but also on the imported content of domestically produced final goods. Antonova, Huxel, Matvieiev and Müller (2025) study the propagation of tariff shocks on U.S. output and inflation using a New Keynesian model with a production network and find stagflationary effects associated with the 2025 tariffs. Our paper contributes to this literature in two ways. First, we develop a framework to quantify the effect of tariff changes on sectoral and aggregate final consumption prices by combining detailed good-level import data with an input-output structure that captures both direct and indirect exposure to tariffs."
R4-009,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,umption prices by combining detailed good-level import data with an input-output structure that captures both direct and indirect exposure to tariffs.
R4-010,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"umption prices by combining detailed good-level import data with an input-output structure that captures both direct and indirect exposure to tariffs. Relative to reduced-form studies of retail pass-through, our approach is designed to measure economy-wide price exposure, including the role of intermediates in transmitting tariff shocks downstream. Second, we focus on the recent U.S.-Canada tariff episode and the Canadian retaliatory measures. Paper Outline The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 outlines the our framework and Section 3 describes the data. Later, Section 4.1 presents measures of tariff elasticities at the good level and discusses how to decompose these elasticities into direct and indirect effects. Section 4.2 examines different counterfactual tariff scenarios. Finally, Section 5 concludes. 2 Framework 2.1 Setup We model the Canadian economy as a static small open economy in which final consumption goods are produced by bundling intermediate inputs from S sectors. A competitive final goods sector operates with the technology 5"
R4-011,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"h final consumption goods are produced by bundling intermediate inputs from S sectors. A competitive final goods sector operates with the technology 5 Y = S ∏ s=1 yγs s , (1) where γs is the share of sector s goods in the final consumption basket and ∑S s=1 γs = 1. Representative producers in each intermediate goods sector source inputs from domestic intermediate sectors, from the U.S., or from the rest of the world (ROW) to produce using a Cobb-Douglas technology: ys = ∏ g x ωROW s, g ROW, s, g !αROW s × x1−αCA s −αUS s −αROW s Others, s (2) where xCA, s, s′ denotes the quantity of intermediate inputs purchased by sector s from domestic sector s′, ωCA s, s′ is the corresponding share within domestic input purchases, and ∑S s′=1 ωCA s, s′ = 1 for each s. Likewise, xUS, s, g denotes the quantity of imported intermediate good g from the U.S. used by sector s, with within-U.S.-imports shares ωUS s, g satisfying ∑G g=1 ωUS s, g = 1 for each s; xROW, s, g denotes the quantity of imported intermediate good g from the rest of the world, with ∑G g=1 ωROW s, g = 1; and xOthers, s denotes all the other inputs (e.g., labor and other operating costs) used by sector s. The parameters αCA s , αUS s , and αROW s are the sector-s expenditure (cost) shares on domestic, U.S.-imported, and ROW-imported intermediate input bundles, respectively. Indices s, s′ ∈{1, . . . , S} refer to sectors and g ∈{1, . . . , G} refers to the types of imported goods."
R4-012,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"intermediate input bundles, respectively. Indices s, s′ ∈{1, . . . , S} refer to sectors and g ∈{1, . . . , G} refers to the types of imported goods. 2.2 Implied Price Effects Under the Cobb-Douglas aggregation technology, the cost minimization would imply that the changes in the price of a sector s, denoted by ∆ps, can be decomposed as the sum of changes in the sector’s markup over costs, ∆µs, and changes in the sector’s 6 costs, which can be further decomposed into changes in non-intermediate input costs, ∆pOthers, s, changes in the price of imported inputs from the rest of the world ∆pROW, g and from the U.S. ∆pUS, g, and changes in the cost of intermediate inputs from other domestic sectors s′, ∆ps′. We assume that (i) markups remain constant, and the tariffs are fully passed through to input costs (∆µs = 0); that (ii) non-intermediate input and the imported goods from the rest of world costs are not affected by tariffs (∆pOthers, s = 0); that (iii) there is no change in the cost of importing goods from countries other than the U.S. ∆pROW, g = 0; and that (iv) firms do not substitute the mix of intermediate goods, or the sourcing of their U.S. imported inputs. In this way, we can isolate the cost pass-through to sectoral prices from Canadian tariffs on U.S.-imported goods."
R4-013,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"cing of their U.S. imported inputs. In this way, we can isolate the cost pass-through to sectoral prices from Canadian tariffs on U.S.-imported goods. General equilibrium effects could attenuate this response, e.g., as firms adjust their input demand or households rebalance their consumption basket. Therefore, the measure we provide below offers an upper bound on the effects of tariffs on sectoral prices and inflation. Sector-Level Price Changes Changes in tariffs of U.S. imported good g, ∆τUS g , have a direct impact on the prices of sector s. In addition, there will be a series of indirect effects as changes in the prices of sector s are further passed through to other sectors that utilize sector s’s output as intermediate inputs in their production. Therefore, the effect of increased tariffs on sector-level prices will depend on the extent to which a sector imports intermediate goods (direct effect) and the extent to which a sector supplies intermediate goods to other sectors (indirect effect)."
R4-014,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"h a sector imports intermediate goods (direct effect) and the extent to which a sector supplies intermediate goods to other sectors (indirect effect). More explicitly, the change in price of domestically produced goods in sector s, ∆ps, can be decomposed as ∆ps = αUS s × G ∑ g=1 ωUS s, g∆τUS g | {z } Direct effect from U.S. imports + αCA s × S ∑ s′=1 ωCA s, s′∆ps′ | {z } Indirect effect from domestic production network . (3) 7 Impacts of Changes in Tariffs ∆τg on Aggregate prices With the above equations, we can solve the equilibrium sector-level price changes ∆⃗p ≡[∆p1, ∆p2, . . . , ∆pS] as ∆⃗p = h I −ACA · ΩCA by sector i−1 · h AUS · ΩUS by good i · ∆⃗τUS (4) where I is an identity matrix, ACA and AUS are diagonal matrices with the shares of domestically produced products and U.S. imports in total intermediate inputs for each sector, respectively. Likewise, ΩCA by sector and ΩUS by good are the matrix counterparts of the shares of domestic and imported products used as intermediate inputs (ωCA s, s′ and ωUS s, g), and ∆⃗τUS is a vector with tariff changes at the good-level. Furthermore, we can decompose sectoral price changes into direct and indirect effects. Direct effects account for the direct impact on each sector’s prices arising from the increased cost of intermediate inputs imported into that sector. This is AUS · ΩUS by good · ∆⃗τ."
R4-015,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"rect impact on each sector’s prices arising from the increased cost of intermediate inputs imported into that sector. This is AUS · ΩUS by good · ∆⃗τ. Indirect effects, on the other hand, measure how changes in each sector’s prices are passed through to other sectors that use its goods as intermediate inputs in production. These effects are captured by the Leontief inverse in Eq. (4) and can be simplified to ∆⃗p −AUS · ΩUS by good · ∆⃗τ Having computed the sectoral price changes above, we can then solve for the aggregate price change ∆P as ∆P =⃗γ · ∆⃗p (5) where⃗γ is the vector with the final consumption shares of domestically produced products from each sector. 3 Data To measure the impact of Canadian tariffs on sectoral and aggregate price changes, we draw on two primary data sources: (i) the Symmetric Input-Output tables produced 8 by Statistics Canada, and (ii) firm-level administrative data from Statistics Canada’s National Accounts Longitudinal Microdata File (NALMF) merged with goods-level import data from Trade by Importer Characteristics - Goods (TIC).1 The first allows us to map the production network structure of the Canadian economy comprehensively. The second allows us to identify which goods within each industry are subject to tariffs. This distinction matters because while the production network is defined at the industry level, tariffs are levied at the goods level –requiring a more granular data source to determine tariff exposure across industries. First, the Symmetric Input-Output tables link final and intermediate uses of goods and services across sectors, as well as total intermediate imports by these sectors and the final consumption of domestically produced goods. In particular, we focus on the Input-Output table for 2022, as it is the most recent year with available data."
R4-016,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"umption of domestically produced goods. In particular, we focus on the Input-Output table for 2022, as it is the most recent year with available data. We use these data at basic prices and aggregate industries at the 2-digit NAICS level. Using these data, we can compute the input shares of domestically produced inputs for each sector. Table A1 in Appendix A, summarizes these shares. Second, we merge firm-level administrative data from Statistics Canada’s National Accounts Longitudinal Microdata File (NALMF) and goods-level import data from Trade by Importer Characteristics - Goods (TIC). TIC is compiled by Statistics Canada using administrative records from the Canadian Border Services Agency (CBSA). Using these two data sources for 2022, we compile information on the universe of firms operating in Canada, as well as their imports at the good level. Goods imports are classified under the Harmonized System (HS) at the 8-digit level (tariff line level), allowing us to map specific tariff policies to our framework in detail."
R4-017,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"ied under the Harmonized System (HS) at the 8-digit level (tariff line level), allowing us to map specific tariff policies to our framework in detail. In addition to the results we present in the following section, we also provide data that may be of independent interest. We compile the shares of U.S.-imported goods used as intermediate inputs for each industry. Industries are presented at the 2-digit NAICS level, and goods are summarized using the sections of the Canadian Export 1Statistics Canada. Table 36-10-0001-01 Symmetric input-output tables, detail level. Available at: https: //doi.org/10.25318/3610000101-eng 9 Classification. Using this level of aggregation, Table 1 provides import data for 20 sectors and 21 goods categories. 4 Quantifying the Aggregate Price Effects of Tariffs on Canada’s Imports from the U.S. 4.1 Heterogeneous Impacts Across Imported Goods There is substantial variation in imported goods across sectors, implying heterogeneity in their exposure to changes in costs or tariffs. Furthermore, sectors vary widely in their linkages to other sectors. In this context, it is useful to understand how sensitive final consumption prices are to changes in good-specific tariffs, and how those tariffs are passed through via the domestic production network. For each type of good, we construct a measure of the elasticity of the price of final consumption with respect to a 1 percent change in tariffs on U.S. imports in that good. Figure 1 reports these elasticities for each corresponding good category and its decomposition between direct and indirect effects."
R4-018,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,orts in that good. Figure 1 reports these elasticities for each corresponding good category and its decomposition between direct and indirect effects.
R4-019,R4,Bank of Canada (2026). The production network amplification of retaliatory tariffs (Staff Working Paper 2026-35),https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/swp2026-35.pdf,Bank of Canada terms: reuse with attribution,"orts in that good. Figure 1 reports these elasticities for each corresponding good category and its decomposition between direct and indirect effects. Tariffs on machinery and appliances, and transport equipment have the greatest impact. A 1 percentage point increase in tariffs on these goods roughly leads to a 0.05 percentage point increase in the price of final consumption in both cases. Consequently, a 25 percent tariff on these goods generates a 1.2 percentage points (25% × 0.05) increase in the prices of final consumption. The higher elasticity to tariffs on U.S. machinery and appliances, and transport equipment arises from significant reliance on U.S. imports (with average import shares of 24 percent and 14 percent across Canadian industries, respectively). However, these import shares do not fully explain the outsized effects of tariffs on machinery and transport goods. Supply chain linkages determine a significant portion of the total effects. Mineral products have an average import share very similar 10 Table 1: U.S."
